// Class 12 English Core practice bank, part 2: Flamingo prose and poetry (The Last Lesson to My Mother at Sixty-six).
StudyData.addPractice(12, "english", {
  "the-last-lesson": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Through whose eyes is ‘The Last Lesson’ told?", options: ["M. Hamel’s","Old Hauser’s","Franz’s","The author’s, as an outside observer"], answer: 2, explain: "The story is a first-person account by the schoolboy Franz.", level: 1 },
      { q: "For how long had M. Hamel been teaching in the village school?", options: ["Forty years","Four years","Fourteen years","Four months"], answer: 0, explain: "He had taught there for forty years, which makes the last day so emotional.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which two regions were affected by the order from Berlin?", options: ["Normandy and Brittany","Paris and Lyon","Prussia and Bavaria","Alsace and Lorraine"], answer: 3, explain: "Only German was to be taught in the schools of Alsace and Lorraine.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which grammar topic had Franz failed to prepare for the day?", options: ["Tenses","Participles","Prepositions","Punctuation"], answer: 1, explain: "He had not learnt the rule on participles and feared a scolding.", level: 1 },
      { q: "M. Hamel’s fine Sunday clothes on an ordinary school day show that he:", options: ["was expecting an inspector","was going to a wedding","wanted to honour the last lesson","had no other clothes"], answer: 2, explain: "The special dress shows respect for the occasion, his language and his pupils.", level: 2 },
      { q: "What does the presence of Old Hauser and other villagers at the back of the room suggest?", options: ["They regretted the schooling they had missed and honoured the teacher","They had come to inspect the school","They wanted to learn German","They had been punished by the teacher"], answer: 0, explain: "Old Hauser followed the lesson from a primer, showing regret for lost chances and respect for M. Hamel.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Whom does M. Hamel blame for the neglect of French?", options: ["Only the pupils","Only the Prussians","Only the parents","Pupils, parents and himself"], answer: 3, explain: "He says everyone, including himself, had put off learning and teaching French.", level: 2 },
      { q: "M. Hamel describes French as:", options: ["the language of the conquerors","the clearest and most logical language","a difficult and old-fashioned language","a language only for scholars"], answer: 1, explain: "He praises French as the most beautiful, clearest and most logical language in the world.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Franz wonders whether the pigeons will also be made to coo in German. What does this thought reveal?", options: ["He had become a good German scholar","He loved pigeons more than lessons","He feels the order is absurd and impossible to enforce on everything","He hoped the order would be cancelled"], answer: 2, explain: "The childlike remark shows how senseless it is to force a language on a people.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In the story, M. Hamel says that a people who keep hold of their language are as if they hold the key to their prison. The idea behind this is that:", options: ["a shared language keeps identity and hope of freedom alive","language is only a means of trade","prisoners should learn French","learning many languages is useless"], answer: 0, explain: "A conquered people who preserve their language preserve their culture and the means of becoming free again.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Franz understands the lesson more easily on the last day than ever before.\nReason (R): He listens with full attention because he knows he will never get another chance.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "His concentration, born of regret and the sense of an ending, makes everything seem clear. This is the irony of the story.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "At the end, M. Hamel writes on the blackboard instead of speaking. This is best explained by the fact that he:", options: ["had lost his voice through illness","wished to teach spelling one last time","was afraid the Prussian soldiers would hear him","was too overcome with emotion to say the words"], answer: 3, explain: "Pale and choked with feeling, he could not speak, so he wrote ‘Vive la France!’ and dismissed the class.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What lesson was Franz supposed to recite, and how did he fare?", a: "He was supposed to recite the rule for participles. He could not say a word, because he had not learnt it.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why did Franz think of staying away from school that morning?", a: "The day was bright and warm and the birds and soldiers drilling outside tempted him; he also feared being scolded about participles.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "How did Franz’s attitude to his lessons and to M. Hamel change in the course of the morning?", a: "At first Franz was careless and resented lessons, books and the strict teacher. After hearing the order from Berlin he felt ashamed of his neglect. He now valued his books, forgot the ruler and bad temper, and felt pity and respect for M. Hamel.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Why did the villagers come to sit in the classroom on the last day?", a: "The villagers, led by Old Hauser, came to thank M. Hamel for forty years of service and to show respect for their language. They also regretted their own lack of schooling and wished to learn what they could before French was banned.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "How does the author show M. Hamel’s devotion to his work and his pupils on the last day?", a: "M. Hamel dressed in his best clothes to honour the day. He taught with unusual patience and explained everything clearly. He praised French, blamed himself for past neglect, and was so moved at the end that he could only write ‘Vive la France!’ on the board.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "‘When people lose their language, they lose their identity.’ How does ‘The Last Lesson’ support this statement?", a: "The story shows that a language carries a people’s culture, pride and freedom.\n1. The Prussian order to teach only German in Alsace and Lorraine is an attempt to erase the identity of the conquered.\n2. M. Hamel calls French the clearest, most logical language and says that a people who hold to their language hold the key to their prison.\n3. Old Hauser and the villagers attend the lesson out of regret, showing they now see what they have neglected.\n4. Franz suddenly values the language he had taken for granted.\n5. Even the pigeons remark highlights the absurdity of imposing a language by force.\nThus the story presents the mother tongue as the keeper of identity, memory and hope of freedom.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Value-based: Franz put off his lessons, saying he had plenty of time. Many students delay work in the same way. What lessons about time, education and one’s own language can we draw from the story? Answer in 120–150 words.", a: "Franz’s story teaches us not to postpone what matters, because opportunities can vanish suddenly.\n1. Time: Franz always said he would learn ‘tomorrow’, but tomorrow never came. Delay turns into regret.\n2. Education: books and teachers, which seemed a burden, become precious once lost. We should value the chance to learn while we have it.\n3. Shared responsibility: M. Hamel blames parents, pupils and himself, showing that everyone must support learning.\n4. Mother tongue: we must read, speak and write our own language with pride, since it keeps culture and identity alive.\n5. Respect for teachers: Franz understands his teacher’s love only at the end; we should show gratitude in time.\nA wise student acts today, and values language, learning and teachers before they are taken away.", marks: 5, type: "hots" },
      { q: "Case study: M. Hamel tells the class that the people of Alsace have always put off learning till tomorrow, and that this is why the Prussians can say they are not French. He adds that the fault lies with the parents, who preferred sending children to work on farms and in mills, and also with himself, since he sent pupils to water his plants and gave holidays when he wished to go fishing.\n(i) What habit of the Alsatians does M. Hamel criticise?\n(ii) Name two other persons or groups he blames.\n(iii) What does his readiness to blame himself show about his character?", a: "(i) He criticises their habit of postponing their lessons until tomorrow.\n(ii) He blames the parents, who cared more for earning money from their children’s work, and himself, for using pupils for personal errands and giving holidays.\n(iii) It shows his honesty, humility and sense of responsibility; he does not blame others alone.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "lost-spring": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "What does the name Saheb-e-Alam mean?", options: ["Lord of the universe","Child of the street","Friend of the poor","King of the rubbish heap"], answer: 0, explain: "The grand meaning of the name contrasts sadly with the boy’s poverty.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Where in relation to Delhi is Seemapuri?", options: ["In the heart of the city, near the Red Fort","In Dhaka, Bangladesh","Near Firozabad","On the outskirts of Delhi"], answer: 3, explain: "Seemapuri is a settlement on the edge of Delhi where migrant families such as Saheb’s live.", level: 1 },
      { q: "For the children of the ragpickers, garbage is sometimes like:", options: ["a toy shop","gold","a source of fear","a classroom"], answer: 1, explain: "A coin or a useful object found in the rubbish feels like a treasure to them.", level: 1 },
      { q: "What was the family trade of Mukesh?", options: ["Weaving carpets","Repairing cars","Making glass bangles","Selling tea"], answer: 2, explain: "Mukesh belongs to a family of bangle-makers in Firozabad.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Why do the bangle-makers of Firozabad often lose their eyesight early?", options: ["They work in dark, badly ventilated hovels with glass and heat","They read too much","They never wash their faces","They walk in strong sunlight all day"], answer: 0, explain: "They work in dim, hot, poorly ventilated rooms near furnaces, which damages their eyes.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which two ‘vicious circles’ trap the bangle-makers?", options: ["Hunger and thirst","Floods and drought","Schooling and exams","Exploitation by moneylenders, middlemen and officials, and the burden of caste"], answer: 3, explain: "They are caught between exploitation by the sahukars, middlemen and authorities and the caste into which they were born.", level: 2 },
      { q: "What reason does Mukesh’s grandmother give for the family’s work in the bangle trade?", options: ["It is the only job that pays well","It is their destiny, given by God","The government forced them","They enjoy it"], answer: 1, explain: "She accepts it as their lot, a fatalism that stops families from imagining another life.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The writer offers to start a school for Saheb. His reaction and her own comment suggest that:", options: ["a school was already open nearby","Saheb was eager to leave the city","her promise was lightly made and not likely to be kept soon","the school would be free of fees"], answer: 2, explain: "Saheb treats it as a real offer, but the writer admits it was a promise like many made and not kept.", level: 2 },
      { q: "At the tea stall, the steel canister Saheb carries seems heavier to him than his plastic bag. This contrast suggests that he has:", options: ["gained a salary but lost his freedom and carries a burden","become stronger","been promoted","left the city"], answer: 0, explain: "Although he now earns, he is no longer his own master; the canister symbolises the new weight of dependence.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which feature of Mukesh sets him apart from many children of Firozabad?", options: ["He is richer than the others","He refuses to work","He knows how to read English","He dares to dream of a different life"], answer: 3, explain: "Unlike others who accept their fate, Mukesh dreams of becoming a motor mechanic.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): ‘Lost Spring’ is an apt title for the lesson.\nReason (R): Spring stands for childhood, which is lost to poverty and child labour.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "The title is a metaphor: the season of growth and joy stands for the childhood the children never get to enjoy.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A young girl in a Firozabad workshop joins pieces of glass over a flame, not knowing what the bangles stand for in a bride’s life. The irony lies in the fact that:", options: ["she is paid too much for her work","the bangle is a symbol of married happiness, yet the girl making it knows nothing of its meaning","she has seen many weddings","she wears many bangles herself"], answer: 1, explain: "Bangles are a sacred sign of a woman’s married life, but those who make them live in darkness and poverty, outside that world of joy.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Name the two ‘vicious circles’ in which the bangle-makers of Firozabad are caught.", a: "The first is the circle of poverty and exploitation, in which sahukars, middlemen, police and officials exploit them; the second is the circle of caste and tradition, which makes them accept the family trade.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "How did the homes of the ragpickers in Seemapuri look?", a: "They were mud structures with roofs of tin or tarpaulin, with no sewage, drainage or running water.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Describe the living conditions of the people of Seemapuri.", a: "Seemapuri lies on the edge of Delhi. The families, who came from Bangladesh after storms destroyed their homes and fields, live in mud huts with tin or tarpaulin roofs. There is no sewage, drainage or running water. Ragpicking supports them, and they have no papers or security.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Why does the writer say that Saheb’s job at the tea stall is not a real gain for him?", a: "At the tea stall Saheb gets a regular wage and a meal, but he loses the freedom he enjoyed as a ragpicker. He is no longer his own master. The steel canister he carries feels heavier than his old plastic bag, showing his new burden.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the working conditions of the glass-bangle workers of Firozabad.", a: "Families work in dark, dingy, poorly ventilated hovels beside hot furnaces. Children as well as adults work long hours, joining and welding glass. The heat, dust and dim light harm their eyes, and many lose their sight early. Their work is dangerous and poorly paid.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Compare the lives and dreams of Saheb and Mukesh. What do their stories together tell us about childhood in poverty?", a: "Saheb and Mukesh are both children robbed of their spring.\n1. Saheb, a ragpicker from Dhaka living in Seemapuri, wanders barefoot in search of gold in the garbage; later he works at a tea stall and loses his freedom.\n2. Mukesh, born into a bangle-making family of Firozabad, works in dark hovels that damage the eyes.\n3. Saheb has no hope of schooling; the writer’s offer remains only a promise.\n4. Mukesh, though trapped by poverty, caste and fate, dreams of becoming a motor mechanic.\n5. Together they show that poverty, exploitation and tradition take away children’s right to play and learn. Yet dreams like Mukesh’s keep hope alive, and remind readers that society must act to restore what these children have lost.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Value-based: Child labour is illegal in India, yet thousands of children like Saheb and Mukesh work. What can society and each one of us do to give such children back their childhood? Answer in 120–150 words.", a: "Child labour continues because of poverty, exploitation and indifference, so the response must involve everyone.\n1. Government: enforce laws strictly, run schools with free meals and books, and support poor families with jobs and credit so children need not earn.\n2. Society: end the hold of middlemen and moneylenders through cooperatives and fair wages for workers.\n3. Citizens: do not employ children as domestic help or at tea stalls, and report violations to the authorities or helplines.\n4. Students: volunteer to teach children in nearby settlements, share books and uniforms, and raise awareness.\n5. Attitude: stop treating child labour as fate; as Mukesh shows, a child who is given a chance can dream of a better future.\nOnly when every child is in school can the lost spring be restored.", marks: 5, type: "hots" },
      { q: "Case study: Mukesh lives in a lane of Firozabad where the houses have crumbling walls, wobbly doors and no windows. Families are crowded together with stray animals, and the lanes stink of garbage. He tells the writer that he will become a motor mechanic and will walk to the garage to learn. She notes that his dream seems to be a spark in a place where most people have given up hoping.\n(i) How does the writer describe the neighbourhood in which Mukesh lives?\n(ii) What does Mukesh want to become?\n(iii) How is Mukesh’s attitude different from that of the other bangle-makers?", a: "(i) It is a crowded, dirty lane with crumbling walls, wobbly doors, no windows and garbage everywhere.\n(ii) He wants to become a motor mechanic.\n(iii) The others accept bangle-making as their destiny and lack the courage to dream, while Mukesh dares to hope for a different future.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "deep-water": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "‘Deep Water’ is best described as:", options: ["a short story by an Indian writer","a poem about the sea","a speech on swimming","an autobiographical account of the author’s own experience"], answer: 3, explain: "William O. Douglas narrates a true incident from his own childhood.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Who ran the swimming pool at Yakima where the incident took place?", options: ["The school","The YMCA","A private club","The town council"], answer: 1, explain: "It was the pool of the YMCA at Yakima.", level: 1 },
      { q: "What did the big boy do to Douglas at the pool?", options: ["He taught him to float","He saved him","He threw him into the deep end","He stole his clothes"], answer: 2, explain: "The older boy threw him in as a prank, which nearly drowned him.", level: 1 },
      { q: "When he sank to the bottom, Douglas planned to:", options: ["spring up from the bottom to reach the surface","shout for help immediately","float quietly","swim to the far wall underwater"], answer: 0, explain: "He decided to touch the bottom and push himself up, but panic and weakness prevented it.", level: 1 },
      { q: "How did the near-drowning change Douglas’s way of life for years?", options: ["He became a champion swimmer","He moved to California","He disliked the YMCA only","Fear of water ruined his fishing and canoeing trips"], answer: 3, explain: "The terror returned whenever he went into lakes and rivers, spoiling his enjoyment of outdoor pleasures.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Why did the instructor train Douglas one step at a time?", options: ["He had no time","To rebuild confidence and skill gradually","Because the pool was closed","Because Douglas disliked swimming"], answer: 1, explain: "He used a belt and rope on a pulley, then worked on breathing with the face underwater and on kicking, so confidence grew with each skill.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Even after months of lessons, fear gripped Douglas when he was in water alone. This shows that:", options: ["his instructor was poor","he disliked the lake","the fear lay in his mind as well as in his muscles","he had forgotten the strokes"], answer: 2, explain: "Training the body was not enough; he had to master the terror in his mind.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which famous remark about fear does Douglas recall at the end?", options: ["Nothing to fear but fear itself","Fear is a friend","Fear makes us wise","Face the fear and run"], answer: 0, explain: "He recalls Roosevelt’s line that the only thing we have to fear is fear itself.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Douglas says his will to live grew stronger after he conquered his fear of water. This shows that:", options: ["he never felt fear again","swimming cures every problem","fear is useful in some cases","conquering a deep fear gives new energy and freedom"], answer: 3, explain: "Having overcome terror, he felt free to walk the trails and climb the peaks.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Why did Douglas’s attempt to spring up from the bottom fail?", options: ["The pool was too shallow","Panic and exhaustion left his legs without strength","The big boy held him down","He hit the wall"], answer: 1, explain: "Terror paralysed him, so he could not push himself up.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The instructor’s lessons alone did not cure Douglas of his fear of water.\nReason (R): The fear was lodged in his mind and needed to be faced by his own willpower.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "The instructor made the body skilled, but Douglas had to overcome the mental fear himself by swimming alone.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Which quality of Douglas is most clearly shown by his decision to return to the water after years of terror?", options: ["Carelessness","Jealousy","Determination","Pride"], answer: 2, explain: "He refused to let fear rule his life and worked to defeat it.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What lasting effect did the near-drowning have on Douglas?", a: "It left him with a deep terror of water that lasted for years and spoilt his enjoyment of swimming, canoeing and fishing.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Who helped Douglas overcome his fear, and how long did the training last?", a: "A swimming instructor helped him, working patiently with him for several months.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Describe Douglas’s near-drowning experience at the YMCA pool.", a: "While Douglas was alone at the pool, a big bully threw him into the deep end. He swallowed water and sank, and tried to spring up from the bottom, but his legs failed. He went down three times, almost lost consciousness, and was finally rescued.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "How did the instructor teach Douglas to swim?", a: "The instructor worked slowly and patiently, teaching each part separately. He first moved Douglas across the pool in a belt tied to a rope and pulley, then taught him to put his face underwater and breathe, and to kick. Finally he joined the pieces together. This gradual approach rebuilt his confidence and made his body master the strokes.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Why did the fear return when Douglas tried to swim alone, and how did he finally defeat it?", a: "Even after months of training, terror returned in the lake because the fear was in his mind. Douglas faced it again and again, swimming alone in the pool, across a lake and finally across a mountain lake, until he felt free. He realised that fear itself was the real enemy.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "‘The only thing we have to fear is fear itself.’ Discuss this idea in the light of ‘Deep Water’.", a: "Douglas’s story shows that fear is often a greater enemy than the danger itself.\n1. As a child he was knocked over by waves at a beach and later nearly drowned at the YMCA pool.\n2. The terror that followed spoiled his love of fishing, canoeing and swimming for years.\n3. Training by a patient instructor improved his skill, but the fear continued to grip him in lakes.\n4. Douglas realised that fear lived in his mind and had to be faced directly, so he went back to the water again and again.\n5. When he finally swam freely he felt released, and his will to live grew stronger.\nThus the lesson teaches that determination and facing a fear are the only cure.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Value-based: Many students are afraid of exams, public speaking or new challenges. Using Douglas’s experience, explain how fear can be overcome and what part teachers and family can play. Answer in 120–150 words.", a: "Douglas’s experience offers a practical way to deal with fear.\n1. Face the fear: avoiding water made his terror grow; only returning to it set him free. Students should prepare for and face exams or stage fright instead of running from them.\n2. Go step by step: the instructor taught one skill at a time. Breaking a big task into small parts builds confidence.\n3. Practise: repeated practice makes the body and mind comfortable.\n4. Willpower: after skills come, the final battle is in the mind; one must decide not to be ruled by fear.\n5. Support: a patient teacher, like the instructor, and encouraging parents give courage without ridicule.\nA student who follows these steps can turn fear into strength and carry that confidence into every area of life.", marks: 5, type: "hots" },
      { q: "Case study: As a small boy Douglas loved water, but at a beach in California he was knocked down by a wave and frightened. Some years later, wanting to swim, he joined the pool at the YMCA in Yakima, which he thought was safe. Even so, he could not entirely trust the water.\n(i) What first frightened the young Douglas about water?\n(ii) Why did he choose the YMCA pool?\n(iii) What does this passage show about his attitude to swimming?", a: "(i) A wave at a California beach knocked him down and scared him.\n(ii) He felt a pool was a safe place to learn to swim.\n(iii) He loved water and wanted to swim but still carried some fear, which shows both his interest and the lingering anxiety that later became terror.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "the-rattrap": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "What did the peddler make and sell for a living?", options: ["Iron tools","Wire rattraps","Leather boots","Christmas decorations"], answer: 1, explain: "He made small rattraps of wire and sold them, begging and stealing when trade was poor.", level: 1 },
      { q: "What did the peddler believe the whole world to be?", options: ["A garden","A school","A rattrap","A market"], answer: 2, explain: "He thought the world offered baits such as riches and comfort to trap people.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Where did the peddler lose his way after stealing the crofter’s money?", options: ["In a forest","In a desert","In a city","On a ship"], answer: 0, explain: "He wandered in a dark forest and could not find the road, as if trapped.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Who invited the peddler to spend Christmas at the manor?", options: ["The crofter","The sheriff","The master blacksmith","Edla, the ironmaster’s daughter"], answer: 3, explain: "Edla pitied him and persuaded him to stay.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Why did the old crofter treat the peddler kindly?", options: ["He was afraid of him","He was lonely and glad of company","He wanted to buy a rattrap","He knew the peddler was rich"], answer: 1, explain: "The crofter shared his food and talk, welcoming the visitor in his lonely life.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Why did the ironmaster at first welcome the peddler warmly?", options: ["He recognised the peddler as a thief","He wanted to buy rattraps","He mistook him for an old comrade, Nils Olof","The peddler was carrying a letter"], answer: 2, explain: "In the dim light of the forge the ironmaster took the ragged stranger for a friend from his regiment.", level: 2 },
      { q: "How did Edla react when her father threatened to call the sheriff after finding out who the peddler really was?", options: ["She asked that the man be allowed to stay as their Christmas guest","She agreed with him","She left the house","She gave the peddler money and sent him away"], answer: 0, explain: "Edla insisted that the poor man should have a peaceful Christmas with them.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which feature of Edla most influenced the peddler?", options: ["Her wealth","Her anger","Her education","Her kindness and trust"], answer: 3, explain: "She treated him with respect and trust, which stirred his conscience.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In the forest the peddler feels that he is walking in circles and cannot get out. The writer uses this to show that:", options: ["he was a poor navigator","he himself had fallen into the trap of temptation","the forest was owned by the crofter","he had lost his rattraps"], answer: 1, explain: "The stolen money tempted him, and the forest becomes his own rattrap.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The peddler’s change of heart was due more to Edla’s kindness than to the ironmaster’s hospitality.\nReason (R): The ironmaster’s welcome was based on a mistaken identity, while Edla knew the truth and still trusted him.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Real kindness given to a person known to be a vagrant touched him deeply, unlike the earlier invitation made by mistake.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "The peddler’s Christmas gift to Edla was a rattrap with the stolen money and a letter. The gift is ironical because:", options: ["rattraps were hard to make","Edla disliked rattraps","the symbol of the world’s temptation is returned by a man who has escaped it","the crofter had no use for it"], answer: 2, explain: "The trap that once stood for the cruel world now stands for the thief’s escape, thanks to goodness.", level: 3 },
      { q: "What is the main message of ‘The Rattrap’?", options: ["Kindness and trust can awaken the goodness in a person","The poor are always dishonest","Strangers should never be trusted","Christmas is a time for gifts"], answer: 0, explain: "Compassion brings out the best in even a petty thief.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Why did the peddler steal the crofter’s money?", a: "He was tempted when the crofter, who was proud of it, showed him his thirty kronor. The peddler, poor and hungry, could not resist the bait.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What did Edla receive as her Christmas gift from the peddler?", a: "She received a small rattrap, along with the stolen money and a letter.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "How did the crofter treat the peddler, and how did the peddler repay him?", a: "The crofter welcomed the stranger, gave him food and shelter and talked to him warmly. He even showed him his thirty kronor. The peddler repaid this hospitality by stealing the money while the old man was away, showing his poverty and lack of conscience at that time.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Why did the ironmaster treat the peddler warmly at first and angrily later?", a: "The ironmaster mistook the ragged peddler for his old regimental comrade Nils Olof and invited him home. Next day, when the peddler was washed and shaved, the ironmaster realised his mistake and was angry because he had been deceived. His kindness depended on appearances.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the peddler’s idea that ‘the whole world is a rattrap’.", a: "The peddler thought the world was like his rattrap: it offers bait such as riches, shelter, food and warmth to attract people. Anyone who touches the bait is caught, and everything ends. His own life of poverty seemed to prove this, until Edla’s kindness changed his view.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Write a character sketch of Edla Willmansson and show how she changes the peddler.", a: "Edla is the kind and sensitive daughter of the ironmaster.\n1. She notices the tired peddler at the ironworks and invites him for Christmas, showing compassion.\n2. She is gentle and understanding, unlike her father, who is proud and judges by appearance.\n3. When she learns that he is a vagrant, she does not turn him away, but asks that he stay as their guest.\n4. She treats him with respect and trust, not as a thief, even though he looks poor and rough.\n5. This unconditional kindness stirs the peddler’s conscience; he returns the stolen money and leaves a rattrap as a gift, calling himself Captain von Stahle.\nThus Edla shows that human goodness and trust can redeem even a thief.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Value-based: The peddler is reformed not by punishment but by kindness. Do you think kindness is more powerful than punishment in changing people? Give reasons with reference to the story and your own life. Answer in 120–150 words.", a: "Yes, I believe kindness often changes people more deeply than punishment does.\n1. In the story the peddler had been shaped by hardship, believing the world cruel. Threat of the sheriff would have confirmed his bitterness.\n2. Edla’s trust made him see himself as a human being worthy of respect, so he returned the money.\n3. Punishment may stop a wrong act through fear, but kindness changes the heart and keeps the person from repeating it.\n4. In everyday life, a teacher who encourages a careless pupil often gets better results than one who only scolds.\n5. However, justice also has a role in serious crimes; kindness should be balanced with fairness.\nOverall, the story convinces us that compassion can bring out the best in people.", marks: 5, type: "hots" },
      { q: "Case study: After stealing the crofter’s money the peddler leaves the road and enters a dark forest. He walks for hours but seems to come back to the same place. Tired and frightened, he feels that the forest is a big rattrap from which he cannot escape, and that he has been caught like the rats he used to trap.\n(i) What had the peddler stolen?\n(ii) How does he describe his situation?\n(iii) What does this episode tell us about the central idea of the story?", a: "(i) He had stolen thirty kronor from the old crofter.\n(ii) He feels the forest is a rattrap from which he cannot escape; he is trapped like a rat.\n(iii) It shows the central idea that the world tempts people with bait, and anyone who gives in, as the peddler did, is caught in a trap of his own making.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "indigo": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Who persuaded Gandhi to visit Champaran?", options: ["Rajkumar Shukla","Rajendra Prasad","C.F. Andrews","Brajkishore Prasad"], answer: 0, explain: "Shukla, a poor peasant, followed Gandhi until he agreed to come.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In which year did Gandhi go to Champaran?", options: ["1857","1930","1947","1917"], answer: 3, explain: "The Champaran satyagraha took place in 1917.", level: 1 },
      { q: "What was the tinkathia system?", options: ["Peasants paid taxes in cash","Peasants had to grow indigo on part of their land for the landlords","Landlords shared profits equally","Peasants were free to grow any crop"], answer: 1, explain: "Under it, a fixed portion of each peasant’s land had to be planted with indigo for the British landlords.", level: 1 },
      { q: "What colour dye does the indigo plant produce?", options: ["Red","Yellow","Blue","Green"], answer: 2, explain: "Indigo gives a blue dye.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Gandhi was ordered to leave Champaran. What did he do?", options: ["Disobeyed the order and prepared to face the court","Left at once","Hid in a village","Went to the Viceroy"], answer: 0, explain: "He refused to leave and accepted the punishment, an act of civil disobedience.", level: 2 },
      { q: "How did the lawyers who were helping Gandhi respond when he said he might go to jail?", options: ["They went home","They asked the court to pardon him","They sought help from the British","They decided to follow him to jail rather than desert the peasants"], answer: 3, explain: "Their decision showed how Gandhi’s example gave them courage.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Why did Gandhi object to C.F. Andrews staying in Champaran to help the lawyers?", options: ["Andrews was ill","He wanted the Indians to fight their own battle and be self-reliant","Andrews opposed Gandhi","The court banned Andrews"], answer: 1, explain: "Gandhi believed that the struggle should depend on Indians, not on a British friend.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Besides the legal case, what did Gandhi arrange in the villages?", options: ["Elections","Factories","Schools, sanitation and health care by volunteers","Banks"], answer: 2, explain: "He combined political work with social work in education, hygiene and health.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In court Gandhi said he had disobeyed the order out of a sense of higher duty. This suggests that for him:", options: ["conscience can be greater than an unjust order","law is never important","officials were his friends","the court was weak"], answer: 0, explain: "He placed the voice of conscience above an order he considered unjust.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Gandhi accepted a refund of only 25 per cent for the peasants.\nReason (R): He felt that the principle that the landlords had to give back part of the money mattered more than the amount, and it broke their prestige.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Gandhi saw that the real victory was moral: the landlords had been forced to yield, and the peasants were freed from fear.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "The officials were puzzled when Gandhi refused to leave but showed no anger. This was because he:", options: ["was a rebel by nature","wanted to go to jail","did not understand the order","combined firm resistance with non-violence and courtesy"], answer: 3, explain: "His peaceful but unbending defiance had no precedent for the authorities.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Why does the writer regard the Champaran episode as a turning point in the freedom struggle?", options: ["It ended British rule","It taught Indians fearlessness and the power of non-violent resistance","It removed all landlords","It started the Salt March"], answer: 1, explain: "It showed that ordinary Indians could stand up to the British and win peacefully.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What was the tinkathia system?", a: "It was a system under which peasants of Champaran had to plant indigo on a fixed part of their land (three out of every twenty parts) and give the crop to the British landlords.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "According to Gandhi, what was the real relief the peasants gained from the Champaran movement?", a: "The real relief was freedom from fear, which gave the peasants courage and dignity.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "How did Rajkumar Shukla persuade Gandhi to visit Champaran?", a: "Shukla, a poor sharecropper from Champaran, met Gandhi at the Lucknow Congress in 1916 and begged him to visit his district. He followed Gandhi to Kanpur, Ahmedabad and Calcutta, never giving up, until Gandhi fixed a date. His persistence won Gandhi’s heart.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Why did Gandhi insist on self-reliance for the peasants of Champaran?", a: "Gandhi felt that the peasants must not depend on outsiders to win their rights. He objected to the English friend C.F. Andrews staying to help, so that Indians would learn to fight their own battle. Self-reliance would give them courage, dignity and lasting strength, and would prepare India for the larger fight for freedom.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "How did Gandhi’s trial at Motihari show his method of civil disobedience?", a: "Gandhi refused to obey the order to leave the district but accepted the legal penalty. Thousands of peasants gathered at the court. Confused officials postponed judgement and then withdrew the case. This showed that non-violent defiance backed by the people could defeat an unjust order.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe how Gandhi’s work in Champaran went beyond a legal fight. What qualities of leadership does it reveal?", a: "Gandhi saw the peasants’ problems as social as well as political.\n1. He gathered thousands of statements from peasants and exposed the injustice of the tinkathia system.\n2. He used civil disobedience, refusing to leave the district when ordered, and the case against him was withdrawn after thousands of peasants gathered at the court in support.\n3. He pressed for a commission of inquiry, of which he was a member, and it found the landlords at fault.\n4. He accepted a 25 per cent refund, valuing the principle over the amount.\n5. He arranged for volunteers to run primary schools and to teach villagers hygiene and health care, so that the peasants’ lives improved as well as their legal position.\nThese acts show truth, courage, practical wisdom, non-violence and concern for the poor. Gandhi’s leadership gave Indians confidence to face the British.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Value-based: Gandhi believed in service and self-reliance. How can young people today apply these values in their own communities? Answer in 120–150 words with reference to the lesson.", a: "Gandhi’s Champaran work shows how personal effort and service can change a community.\n1. Self-reliance: Gandhi refused outside help so that peasants learnt to stand on their own; students should depend on their own effort, not on shortcuts.\n2. Service: volunteers in Champaran taught children and promoted cleanliness; young people can teach in neighbourhood schools, plant trees and keep surroundings clean.\n3. Courage: Gandhi challenged an unjust order peacefully; we too should speak against injustice without violence.\n4. Attention to small things: he insisted that people keep themselves and their homes clean, even in the middle of a political struggle.\n5. Principle over profit: he valued dignity above money, as in accepting 25 per cent.\nBy living these values, young people can make their villages and cities fairer and cleaner.", marks: 5, type: "hots" },
      { q: "Case study: When Gandhi arrived at the court in Motihari, thousands of peasants had gathered around the building. Officials found that they could not control the crowd without Gandhi’s help, and he asked the people to remain peaceful. The magistrate postponed the case and later it was withdrawn.\n(i) What did the gathering of peasants show?\n(ii) What did Gandhi do to help the officials?\n(iii) How did the case end, and what was learnt from it?", a: "(i) It showed that the peasants trusted Gandhi and had lost their fear of the authorities.\n(ii) He asked the crowd to stay calm and peaceful, which helped keep order.\n(iii) The case was withdrawn. It was learnt that peaceful civil disobedience supported by the people can defeat an unjust order.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "my-mother-at-sixty-six": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Where is the poet driving in the poem?", options: ["To a railway station","To a hospital","To her office","To Cochin airport"], answer: 3, explain: "She is travelling by car to Cochin airport after visiting her parents’ home.", level: 1 },
      { q: "How is the poet’s mother sitting in the car?", options: ["Reading a book","Dozing with her mouth open","Looking out of the window","Talking loudly"], answer: 1, explain: "The sight of her sleeping, ashen face disturbs the poet.", level: 1 },
      { q: "What is the form of the poem?", options: ["Sonnet with a fixed rhyme","Ballad with four stanzas","Free verse in a single stanza","Limerick"], answer: 2, explain: "It has no regular rhyme and runs on as a single stanza, like the poet’s flowing thoughts.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The mother’s face is compared to the face of a corpse and also to:", options: ["a late winter’s moon","a morning sun","a blooming lotus","a calm lake"], answer: 0, explain: "Both similes stress paleness and frailty.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Why does the poet look out of the car window?", options: ["To check the traffic","To admire the countryside","To find the airport","To turn away from the painful sight of her mother"], answer: 3, explain: "She looks away to distract herself from her fears.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The sprinting trees and merry children suggest:", options: ["danger on the road","youth, energy and life","the poet’s impatience","the end of summer"], answer: 1, explain: "They are images of vitality that contrast with the mother’s age.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The ‘old familiar ache’ and ‘child’s fear’ recall:", options: ["the poet’s fear of flying","her fear of travelling alone","her childhood dread of losing her mother","the memory of an illness"], answer: 2, explain: "The adult poet again feels the childish fear of separation from the mother.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Why does the poet smile and say ‘see you soon’ at the end?", options: ["To hide her worry and reassure her mother","She is happy to leave","She has forgotten her fear","She is angry"], answer: 0, explain: "The smile is a mask for her sadness and fear.", level: 2 },
      { q: "At the security check the poet sees her mother’s face as pale as the late winter’s moon, wan and fading. What does this image suggest?", options: ["The mother is ready to travel","The mother is healthy","The weather is cold","The mother’s life is nearing its end"], answer: 3, explain: "The fading, colourless moon suggests old age and the nearness of death.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The poet contrasts the trees and children outside with her mother inside the car.\nReason (R): The contrast heightens her sense of her mother’s frailty and approaching old age.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Youth and speed outside make the stillness and paleness inside seem sadder.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "The poet does not speak of her fear to her mother. This silence best shows:", options: ["her coldness","her love and wish not to worry her mother","her lack of time","her dislike of airports"], answer: 1, explain: "She hides her emotion and offers only a smile and a hopeful farewell.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The repeated word ‘smile’ at the end of the poem suggests that the poet’s smile is:", options: ["spontaneous and joyous","a sign of boredom","an effortful pretence covering deep sadness","a sign of victory"], answer: 2, explain: "The repetition shows how hard she works to keep up a cheerful appearance.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Which simile in the poem shows the poet’s fear on seeing her mother?", a: "The poet compares her mother’s face to that of a corpse (ashen), which shows her fear that her mother may die soon.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why does the poet look out of the car at the trees and children?", a: "She looks away to avoid the painful sight of her mother’s pale face and to push her fear out of her mind.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is the ‘old familiar ache’ the poet feels?", a: "It is her childhood fear of losing her mother, which returns when she sees her mother looking frail, pale and old. The poet is now an adult, but the same helpless dread comes back, mingled with love and pain, and she cannot say it aloud.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "What contrast does the poet draw between the world outside and her mother? What is its effect?", a: "Outside, young trees seem to sprint past and happy children spill out of their homes, full of energy and life, as if the whole world is young. Her mother, pale and drowsy, seems almost lifeless. The contrast sharpens the sadness of ageing and the poet’s fear.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "How does the poet hide her feelings at the airport?", a: "After the security check, she looks again at her mother, whose face seems pale like the late winter’s moon. Her heart aches with the old fear, but she hides it. She smiles and says ‘see you soon’, so as not to alarm her mother.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Discuss the use of imagery and simile in ‘My Mother at Sixty-six’ and show how they bring out the theme of ageing and the fear of loss.", a: "Kamala Das uses simple images and similes in a single flowing stanza to convey deep and quiet feeling.\n1. The mother’s face is ashen like a corpse, a simile that shows both her paleness and the poet’s fear of death.\n2. Her open-mouthed dozing is a visual image of weakness and old age.\n3. The sprinting young trees and merry children are images of life and energy, which contrast with the mother.\n4. At the airport the mother looks like the late winter’s moon, wan and fading, suggesting a life near its end.\n5. The poem ends with a forced smile, repeated like a mask, which hides the pain.\nThese devices together express the child’s eternal fear of losing a parent, and the helplessness of loving someone who is growing old.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Value-based: The poem shows a daughter worried about her ageing mother. How should young people care for their ageing parents and grandparents? Answer in 120–150 words with reference to the poem.", a: "Kamala Das reminds us that parents age, and their children must notice and care.\n1. Notice: the poet is shocked to see how frail her mother has become; we must pay attention to our elders’ health, not take them for granted.\n2. Time: busy lives keep children away, but visits and calls matter. A daughter who flies away should leave her mother feeling loved.\n3. Reassurance: the poet’s smile and ‘see you soon’ show a wish to comfort, but real comfort comes from regular presence.\n4. Practical help: take elders for check-ups, help in daily tasks and include them in family life.\n5. Respect: give them dignity and patience, as they once gave us.\nIf we show love in time, we will have fewer regrets later.", marks: 5, type: "hots" },
      { q: "Case study: After the security check, the poet looks again at her mother, who stands at a distance. Her mother, pale like the late winter’s moon, seems small and faded among the crowd. The poet again feels the old pain, but she smiles and waves, saying she will see her soon.\n(i) Whom is the poet looking at, and where is she?\n(ii) Which image describes the mother’s face here?\n(iii) Why does the poet smile and speak cheerfully?", a: "(i) She is looking at her mother, at the airport after the security check.\n(ii) The mother’s face is compared to the late winter’s moon, pale, wan and fading.\n(iii) She smiles to hide her sadness and fear and to reassure her mother that they will meet again soon.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
